{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 22, :comments ["<b>And he lay with Bilhah.</b> He displaced and uprooted her bed from its place when he demanded the insult to his mother, as Rashi explained. And in the vernacular: et arguier. For there are matters written in one context that serve two aspects, this one retrospectively from that one, as I explained above regarding 'and she struck the socket of Jacob's thigh.' 'And Israel heard' — that Reuben displaced his bed — <b>and the sons of Jacob were</b> from then on twelve, and no more. For Jacob did not again have relations with his wives, and this is what Jacob said: 'then you defiled my couch' — meaning, my lying [with her] was removed and ceased. And the verse <b>וישמע</b> was written so that you not wonder when you arrive at 'when you mounted your father's couch,' to say: who told Jacob? Therefore it preceded here and taught you that he heard. Another interpretation: therefore this section was fixed between the matter of Reuben and the matter of the sons of Jacob, to teach you about the verse <b>and the sons of Jacob were twelve</b> — that it does not continue from <b>and he lay with Bilhah</b>, to say that Reuben fathered some of them; rather, it refers to the bearing of Rachel, and at that time 'and the sons of Jacob were twelve,' meaning: he and no other fathered all of them. It repeated the count of Jacob's sons to teach you that he and his twelve sons came to his father."], :book :genesis, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 35, :notes {0 "The phrase בלע״ז ארקוק״א is obscure; read here as Old French 'et arguier' ('and to accuse/dispute'), following the standard identification of this gloss in Rashi's sources."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:07:30.006000444Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}